Version: 1.1
desc: Board Meeting Analog Console by chmaha
tags: console analog saturation transformer glue channel bus master mix colour warmth
author: chmaha
changelog:
  Add full copyright and licence header (upstream authors' notices).
about:
  Instant analog console colour. Put a copy on every channel, on group buses and on the master, and each copy
  behaves like its own strip of an expensive desk.
  Each copy has an input transformer that saturates lows harder (thicker, rounder bass), a gently asymmetric amp
  stage (mostly 2nd and some 3rd harmonic, 2x oversampled with zero latency) and a console EQ curve: subsonic
  high-pass with a low weight bump, a mid voicing and an air shelf. Left and right differ by small component
  tolerances and there is faint frequency-dependent crosstalk.
  Mode: Channel (colour only), Bus (colour plus gentle glue) or Master (subtler colour, more glue). Glue is a
  soft-knee, stereo-linked bus compressor; its amount rises with Drive and the GLUE lamps show the gain reduction.
  Flavour: Warm (iron transformers, thick lows, silky top), Punchy (discrete amps, forward mids, tight lows) or
  Silky (clean VCA path, open air, smooth glue).
  Drive sets how hard the console is pushed. It is gain-compensated, so it changes character, not level.
  Every copy gets its own channel number (CH 07 and so on), shared between copies through gmem and saved with the
  project. The number gives each copy slightly different drive, transformer, EQ and stereo tolerances, like real
  channel strips. Click the CH label for a new character. Duplicated copies sort themselves out automatically.
  Tape (optional) prints the result through subtle tape: head bump, a little low-end tightening, level-dependent
  high-frequency softening and gentle peak rounding. No wow or flutter, so it is safe on every copy.
  Noise (optional) adds a realistic, very low console floor: pink-dominant hiss shaped to 48 Hz-11 kHz at
  -109 dBFS per channel, a warmer partly shared floor on buses (-108 dBFS) and, on the master, amplifier noise
  that rises with the number of channel copies with Noise on. The noise runs steadily while the transport plays
  or records, including silent passages, like powered hardware, and fades out when you stop. Each copy's noise is
  unique, seeded from its channel number. Off by default. Right-click NOISE to choose the floor of that copy
  (RMS per channel): Pristine -109 dBFS (a good console), Vintage -97, Tired -91, Broadcast truck -85, and two
  tape-hiss voicings, brighter and whiter like the medium itself: Cassette -70 and Portastudio -58. On a small
  session, raising it on the Master copy is usually enough.
  Bypass takes the console out with a smooth crossfade for honest comparisons. VU meters read 0 VU at
  -18 dBFS (sine peak). A copy sleeps after one second of silence.
  Original design by chmaha
  Console noise model after CNF Console Noise Floor by Heavy Water Audio (MIT)
  JSFX code released under GPLv3 license
  Claude generated some of this code
